course: dictionary
lesson: 13633
title: Indulge: treats and spoiling
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To indulge means to let yourself have something you enjoy, especially a treat. «Sam decided to indulge in a big slice of chocolate cake.» Sam let himself have something he really wanted, so indulge is about treating yourself.
explain: Indulge can also mean giving someone whatever they want, even when it is not good for them. «Grandma likes to indulge her grandchildren with sweets.» She spoils them, which is kind but maybe too generous. The words around the word give you the clue.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in a simpler phrase such as treat yourself or give in to. If the sentence still makes sense, you have probably found the right meaning.
fact: Indulge is a verb, and its noun is «indulgence».
fact: «Indulge» means to let yourself have a treat, or to give someone what they want.
fact: «Indulge» can also mean to go along with someone's interest, as in «indulge his curiosity».
fact: In history, an «indulgence» in the medieval Catholic Church was a release from punishment for sin, and selling them was one cause of the Reformation.
fact: Useful phrases are indulge in, indulge yourself and indulge a whim.
exercise: «Mum let me indulge in a bowl of ice cream after school.» What does indulge mean here? | To hide from someone | To walk slowly home | To pour out and throw away | *To let yourself have a treat
exercise: Which word is the noun form of indulge? | indulges | *indulgence | indulging | indulged
exercise: «The teacher let the class indulge their curiosity about the stars.» What does indulge their curiosity mean? | Stopped their questions | Made them stay quiet | Hid the stars from them | *Allowed them to follow their interest
test: «Jamie wanted to indulge himself with a long, hot bath.» What does indulge himself mean? | *To treat himself to something he enjoys | To be sad about the day | To clean the bath | To cancel his plans
test: In the medieval Catholic Church, what was an indulgence? | A type of medieval castle | A kind of school exam | *A release from punishment for sin | A bill for a meal
exam: «Dad always indulges the dog with extra biscuits, even though the vet says no.» What does indulges suggest? | Trains the dog to sit | Feeds the dog only fruit | Takes the dog to the vet | *Gives the dog what it wants, even if it is not good for it
